545daa56b3405e22545eeb114b05b5224","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/65d520e545daa56b3405e22545eeb114b05b5224"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/657845?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kenilam","id":657845,"login":"kenilam","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY1Nzg0NQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/c6b6b3927ef2858d8bc186bb5764b8a3a4bb542d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T17:12:49Z","email":"657845+kenilam@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Keni"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T17:12:49Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(server): ask for a password when KICL_GATE_PASSWORD is set (#437)\n\ndev.ki-cl runs on Cloud Run, which has no built-in password option, and\nIAP would mean a Google sign-in plus a load balancer for the custom\ndomain. Basic Auth in the Express server is enough for a dev site.\n\nThe password comes from Secret Manager on the ki-cl-dev service only.\nProduction leaves it unset, so nothing changes there. /health stays\nopen for Cloud Run''s checks. WebSocket upgrades skip Express middleware\nand are still covered by the proxy''s own auth.\n\nCo-authored-by: Keni Lam <admin@loci.world>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"4b2cc531a97d8f3adbe2d0c782fc9d97e576b22b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/git/trees/4b2cc531a97d8f3adbe2d0c782fc9d97e576b22b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/git/commits/c6b6b3927ef2858d8bc186bb5764b8a3a4bb542d","verification":{"payload":"tree 4b2cc531a97d8f3adbe2d0c782fc9d97e576b22b\nparent 8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808\nauthor Keni <657845+kenilam@users.noreply.github.com> 1790788369 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790788369 -0700\n\nfeat(server): ask for a password when KICL_GATE_PASSWORD is set (#437)\n\ndev.ki-cl runs on Cloud Run, which has no built-in password option, and\nIAP would mean a Google sign-in plus a load balancer for the custom\ndomain. Basic Auth in the Express server is enough for a dev site.\n\nThe password comes from Secret Manager on the ki-cl-dev service only.\nProduction leaves it unset, so nothing changes there. /health stays\nopen for Cloud Run''s checks. WebSocket upgrades skip Express middleware\nand are still covered by the proxy''s own auth.\n\nCo-authored-by: Keni Lam <admin@loci.world>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvUMRCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAbOoQABD/FTsVZaHI3cdKt2Ic4p3P\nDr9q3puGju54KAB9mYVCClOZm8L/yeZoes7W8lleoPrAr5zmgyWxbeiZJHsr2DLH\n5EyoxEtkTFKhS3LnJWdXEDIqrU/E/Mv2RTL5ddmIuBgI92QYRevEb3TN8pLXoAzX\nX4texkutJPieU9q+Y8Ky/Wh9du2I3v+eBr+XZEGa0rJTNiDKRPDuAFo6lPocQ2so\n8ZDwVvmA0rHBFFoY6XLM7sjLiW4JQ9o2LPNbMoqPcWLcb+k/b2TMkUse+cDxE/hn\nuNIF183GRQwpc1QMCCYn3S29l0wxUH9n2XB60aC06Hy42LmfwZrfYVfLLWhPxUwp\nrhwfWpaKNBKBdaTcs+UGGFesIh4+be4+jgU8tKkQmn/8IhhF7JQoTNnNHXuvFXpu\nanCZOWXVt1SbOcc+vcrQpKpGhnv9vxyxViqe6JWrWgjhzinb85UOw0vblaqY9Ydv\nQb/Ji4W4aCuiX5JtcxD+cb2lBemSfpaFZskdrcJz94qRpsEX7l6JoBu8CzZm6HGY\nKz7R8GHm5VFYxI8J48JfXAumNX4midcSgNpXoJELGiRJm5TnkPHxCNBd1Wf8EEw/\nykA2W6HmX7SVw5VVWr3B6XPAmFd9vi04WwhQrmviDTTkf39+XIC+oUvXoAc1cN4M\nnHGfkJjnI6gIGd+A9JxM\n=447C\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-30T17:12:50Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/kenilam/Ki.CL/commit/c6b6b3927ef2858d8bc186bb5764b8a3a4bb542d","node_id":"C_kwDOAU3gWtoAKGM2YjZiMzkyN2VmMjg1OGQ4YmMxODZiYjU3NjRiOGEzYTRiYjU0MmQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/kenilam/Ki.CL/commit/8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808","sha":"8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808"}],"sha":"c6b6b3927ef2858d8bc186bb5764b8a3a4bb542d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/c6b6b3927ef2858d8bc186bb5764b8a3a4bb542d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/657845?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/kenilam","id":657845,"login":"kenilam","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY1Nzg0NQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kenilam","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T16:22:47Z","email":"657845+kenilam@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Keni"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T16:22:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs: write the no-custom-CSS, no-wrapper and rethink rules into CLAUDE.md and the Cursor rule (#436)\n\nThese three rules only existed in Claude''s session memory, so they applied to one tool and one machine. Moving them into CLAUDE.md and design-system.mdc makes them load on every session and in Cursor, and puts them under review like the rest of the design rules.\n\nCo-authored-by: Keni Lam <admin@loci.world>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"75d25fdad1626ce333b6187713a7cc0b78e9d8e3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/git/trees/75d25fdad1626ce333b6187713a7cc0b78e9d8e3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/git/commits/8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808","verification":{"payload":"tree 75d25fdad1626ce333b6187713a7cc0b78e9d8e3\nparent 66c4fc9a4e21a47e0bbdd52d8d3150c806713482\nauthor Keni <657845+kenilam@users.noreply.github.com> 1790612567 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790612567 -0700\n\ndocs: write the no-custom-CSS, no-wrapper and rethink rules into CLAUDE.md and the Cursor rule (#436)\n\nThese three rules only existed in Claude''s session memory, so they applied to one tool and one machine. Moving them into CLAUDE.md and design-system.mdc makes them load on every session and in Cursor, and puts them under review like the rest of the design rules.\n\nCo-authored-by: Keni Lam <admin@loci.world>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqupRXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA9+8QADfPQLKPLXySpb22tmrx1zhP\nrbmbIFTfP+dxJU88HDT5Zq/K+hDHprwia+6D39j8xDD/X1SrJVnPsBlGRqxP/mMU\naYB7mRgiTYEewKkYcn/jvXen5FRaR8xWth1/bsl3Eq2T4FloIDuikNndXD8AEaUs\nys47HTWLAqEA14PicxyvPdV3mfFwcENDzt32F/FbfqwfgCnw9ljVTNyv5jyjMBcs\nKuQuBJtQkxqt7GZQUuzDrD+izdC+JZvxtinKS+hRC75b26KSGxEMOMjaBIyFzbAs\nSl1egbUha1BXQsPph/vr95PgqNH5bf140jLiAile6aASV9QY+hmrAHYTZQiLFoSD\nEw5f7AS1gYzQ856KzSN3KnmZKV805/kMoNE9DUEuFa+Hlt7cUPcehacf17TsW8BY\nvrGZvi8g6FeV1Hsv9uDuQMCFzDJQeaXq9or5JQ1wAu5nu5Tj7CXT2rEOOM9nyHKT\n71B+rH/xUhEYB5jrYZ2q/D1ISSDa3Sq8W7LzlWshvkW2sDCYFuIMbcNtE+OpBN9j\nS7RE7FcavBpDIa1GACa2d7qqnPwXvAl98X4kxAOFeMiN+5L9LbhgtH88Ae88VIXg\n3JAXzI9NYARkQK7tUmIXyhJEPd8CRfjxWOiVPl7yXkLt/6u85wumvAMk4kmxzx84\newPSt9RPz/AJDumpJuKm\n=NHvD\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-28T16:22:48Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/kenilam/Ki.CL/commit/8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808","node_id":"C_kwDOAU3gWtoAKDhjNGMzOWY4ODYzM2JmZGMyYTFhMDE4ZWUxN2NkNWIzNTRjNGQ4MDg","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/kenilam/Ki.CL/commit/66c4fc9a4e21a47e0bbdd52d8d3150c806713482","sha":"66c4fc9a4e21a47e0bbdd52d8d3150c806713482","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/66c4fc9a4e21a47e0bbdd52d8d3150c806713482"}],"sha":"8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/8c4c39f88633bfdc2a1a018ee17cd5b354c4d808"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/15368?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/github-actions","id":41898282,"login":"github-actions[bot]","node_id":"MDM6Qm90NDE4OTgyODI=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","type":"Bot","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/commits/66c4fc9a4e21a47e0bbdd52d8d3150c806713482/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-28T07:18:30Z","email":"41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com","name":"github-actions[bot]"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-28T07:18:30Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(factory-arm): add a robot arm experiment that unloads a pallet (#433)\n\n* feat(factory-arm): add a robot arm experiment that unloads a pallet\n\nA new experiment at /experiments/factory-arm: a six-axis arm with a\nvacuum gripper, a stacked pallet, a buffer pallet and an outfeed belt,\nwith physics from @react-three/rapier. Clicking a case queues it for the\nbelt. Cases in the way of lifting it go to the buffer first and follow it\noff last in, first out. The buffer stacks them where they''re supported,\nheavy under light, looking ahead at the cases still to come.\n\nThe arm moves on closed-form IK with speed-limited joints and a wrist\nroll that squares the pad to each case. It goes into and out of contact\nin straight lines, slowing near contact, and swings freely only at\ntravel height.\n\nEight proximity sensors on the links find three obstacles it isn''t told\nabout. Each find rechecks the current move, and a blocked one goes round\nby A* over reachable, clear pad positions. With no way on, a held case\ngoes back to where it was picked; with no way back either, the arm stops\nand the light turns red. The view turns with OrbitControls.\n\nRapier is imported only inside the experiment, not through @/three, so\nits WASM doesn''t load with other pages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): write context refs through the provider\n\noxlint 1.85 from develop flags `react/immutability` when a component\nassigns to `.current` on a ref it got from context, since the compiler\ncan''t tell it''s a ref. The arm, the sensing loop and the runner did that\nfor `command`, `joints`, `held`, `seeing` and `found`, and the case''s\n`useCallback` read `bodies.current`.\n\nThe provider now hands out a `write` object whose functions sit beside\nthe refs they change. `learn` adds new obstacles and bumps `found` once,\nand `track` registers a case''s body and returns what unregisters it.\nComponents still read the refs directly. Nothing else changes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): keep buffer moves off places the arm can''t reach\n\nClicking a buried case sent the cases above it to a buffer spot next to\nthe crate. Once a sensor found the crate, setting the case down there was\nblocked, so the arm carried it back to where it came from. That left the\nclicked case buried again and the rest of the chain failed.\n\nA buffer spot now only counts if the case can go straight down onto it\nclear of every obstacle the arm already knows about. If a spot turns out\nblocked after the move has started, the case goes to the belt instead.\nTaking it back to the pallet is left for when the belt is blocked too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): shorter detours, and notices you can read\n\nThe beam hung over the front of the pallet, in the swing to the buffer,\nso every buffer move went round the back of the cell: about 4.7 m, and 4\nminutes for a nine-move chain. It now hangs low behind the pallet, out of\nthe swing, where it still stops one back-row case being lifted. Average\npaths from the top cases dropped from 4.1 m to 2.3 m, and detours run at\n0.6 m/s instead of 0.35.\n\nA queued case that can''t start because something it needs didn''t move\nwas dropped with nothing on screen. It now shows the same \"Skipped\"\nnotice as a case the arm gives up on.\n\nThe notices sat on the light floor as yellow and grey text. They now use\nthe frosted pane from the image agent''s header, tinted to 85% of the page\ncolour so they read over the cell.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): plan the whole move before picking a case up\n\nThe arm used to find out a case had nowhere to go only once it was\ncarrying it, then took it back or stopped. It now plans every move of a\njob with the obstacles it knows about before it starts, and again when\nthe pad reaches the case, since the sensors may have found more on the\nway. A case in the way tries the best few buffer spots, then the belt.\nIf nothing works it leaves the case where it is, lights the obstacles in\nthe way, says \"No clear path\" and calls off the rest of that click.\n\nA lift is the one move that can''t go round anything, so a click whose\nchain has a lift known to be blocked is refused straight away, and a\nchain found to have one mid-way stops before its next pick. Obstacles\nare only blamed for a move that is otherwise within reach; the travel\nheight is now capped at what the arm can reach above the pick and drop.\n\nCases moved out of the way now stay on the buffer instead of following\nthe clicked case to the belt, and can be clicked like any other.\nObstacles are grey and glow orange while they''re in the way of a move.\nA case queued for the belt glows green, and one being moved out of the\nway glows yellow until it''s on the buffer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): don''t bury queued cases on the buffer\n\nA case moved out of the way could be stacked on a buffer case that was\nitself queued for the belt, so that case then had to be cleared again.\nBuffer spots that would be in the way of lifting any queued case now\nrank after every spot that wouldn''t, and are only used when there''s no\nother room.\n\nThe in-the-way test is the one picking uses (`covers` in cell.ts), so\n\"in the way\" means the same thing on both sides.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): keep carried cases off other cases, and pin notices\n\nA carried case could sweep into a stack and knock it. The planner only\navoided the three obstacles, and once the travel height was capped at the\narm''s reach, a far stack could stand taller than the carried case''s path.\nFree moves now treat the other cases on the pallets as solid too, so a\nway round goes over or past them. Moves into and out of contact still\nignore them, since the lift check already covers those. A box round the\narm and its load skips cases nowhere near, so planning stays around\n20-260 ms a job.\n\nIf the carried case still touches a case it isn''t being set down on, the\narm stops, lifts 10 cm, and plans the rest of the job again once the\nstruck case has settled. With no plan it takes the case back, and with\nno way back it stops red. The struck case glows orange.\n\nThe \"No clear path\" notice is now a warning badge floating over the case\nit''s about, following it if it moves. The page''s hidden live region\nstill announces it, since the scene is one image to a screen reader.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(badge): take meaning from a level prop, as Button does\n\nBadge put how it looks and what it means in one `variant` prop, copied\nfrom shadcn: `destructive`, `positive` and `warning` sat beside\n`secondary`, `outline` and `ghost`. Button already splits these into\n`variant` and `level`, so Badge now does the same.\n\n`variant` keeps `default`, `secondary`, `outline` and `ghost`. `level`\nis typed from Button''s own, `confirm`, `error`, `info` or `warning`: it\nfills the chip with that colour, or edges it for `outline` and `ghost`,\nwhich have no fill. `destructive` is gone, along with its duplicated\nrules. The level rules come after the themes so they win over the\nthemed fills.\n\nThe image agent''s allowance badge was the only one using the old\nvalues, and moves to `level`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(badge): make secondary tell apart from default\n\nSecondary looked the same as default. It now takes a faint kicl-backdrop fill\nand moves the badge''s colour to the text, as Button''s secondary does, so a\nlevel colours the text instead of the fill. Outline and ghost use the page''s\ntext colour. The level rules use two classes so they win over the theme\nrules.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): overhead camera, pallet overflow, and faster moves\n\nThe overhead camera sees obstacles near the pallets before the arm moves,\nso a job with no clear path is refused up front. The \"No clear path\" badge\nsits on the case it refers to.\n\nCases in the way now go to a clear place on the buffer, then on the incoming\npallet, and only bury a queued case when there is no other room. Queued cases\nthat a buffer place would bury are delivered first. Stacks also have to pass\na slenderness check, so the arm doesn''t build towers that tip over. A case\nselected while another is in the air sends that one to a new buffer place\ninstead of the belt.\n\nPlanning a job used to freeze the page for about 2.5s because it searched\nthe full incoming pallet on every job. That search now runs only when no\nbuffer place works, and without look-ahead.\n\nThe arm and gripper are slimmer. The arm swings and descends faster, and\nslows over the last 25cm before it touches a case. The scene has soft\nshadows, and the start view is lower and turned to show the stack and the\nbelt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(status): drop the outer Layout wrapper\n\nThe status div is now the root inside Animation, so a className passed to\nStatus lands on the element that holds the content, with no extra grid\naround it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): movable obstacles, and an arm that recovers on its own\n\nClick an obstacle to select it, then move it with the arrow keys, Shift+Up\nand Down, or the triangles round it. A move is refused if the obstacle would\noverlap a case, pallet, the belt, another obstacle or the arm, so it never\nknocks anything over. The arm knows where a moved obstacle stands.\n\nA case given up on glows red and is tried again once something it plans\nround has changed. A stopped arm checks the same way and carries on when it\nhas a way. The stopped notice is now a badge over the gripper, and both\nnotices are announced to screen readers from hidden live regions.\n\nThe belt has several drop spots, so an obstacle between the arm and the\nusual one no longer blocks every delivery. A spot whose set-down is blocked\nis skipped before any route search. A* weights the distance left more and\nhas a 200ms limit, so a search that finds nothing can''t freeze the page.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(components): add Segmented, and fix Select and CardFooter\n\nSegmented is a one-of-a-few choice on a shared track, built on native\nradios. The highlight slides to the chosen option with CSS anchor\npositioning, and without anchor support the chosen option is filled\ninstead.\n\nSelect centres its text in the styleable select, where the picker icon made\nthe box taller than a line and the text sat at the top. CardFooter renders a\nfooter element with padding top and bottom.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): setup panel, presets, second stack and activity log\n\nA panel over the stage sets up each run. Presets (built in, or saved in\nthis browser) run with a play button, and the active one is edged green.\nThe Manual tab drops obstacles onto the stage, sets the case layers and\nseed, and turns on a second incoming stack. Unsaved changes offer to save\nas a new preset, and starting over while the arm works asks first. The Log\ntab lists every step, coloured as the cases glow, with earlier runs folded\naway. The panel folds away on small screens and on desktop, and the camera\nkeeps the cell centred beside it.\n\nObstacles can be removed from a button over them, and the arm keeps its\nlinks off the cases it reaches over, clearing them first. Cases in the way\ngo only to the buffer. Waiting cases keep what blocks them lit.\n\nThis planner has reached its limits; a new engine follows.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): a new engine that plans each job whole\n\nThe old planner planned one move at a time against a physics world that\nkept shifting under it, and could end up holding a case with nowhere to\ntake it. The new engine, in engine/, models the cell as boxes with kinematic\ncases, and plans a whole job before the arm moves: each case in the way to\na stable place on the buffer, then the asked case to the belt. A refusal\nnames the case, why, and which obstacles are in the way.\n\nThe runner''s rules live in a state machine outside React (conductor.ts),\nwith tests. Plans are made in two web workers, so a slow one never drops\nframes. Collision checks skip links far from a solid, which cut the slowest\nplan from 2.2 s to 254 ms with the same results.\n\nThe arm plans round what the overhead camera and its sensors have seen, and\nfeels an obstacle neither saw. While an obstacle is moved it keeps going as\nlong as its way stays clear. Queued cases in the way go straight to the\nbelt, cases short of buffer room wait their turn, and refused cases are\ntried again after each drop. The shoulder, elbow and wrist now move\ntogether, so the pad no longer sags near full reach.\n\nThe log and the last run are kept in this browser, and the page opens on\nthe last run. The old engine still runs with ?classic, until it''s removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(factory-arm): remove the first engine\n\nThe new engine plans each job whole and has replaced it, so ?classic is\ngone, along with scene/grasp and the physics-driven cases in scene/boxes.\nThe pieces the new engine still used move with it: the arm''s collision\nmodel to engine/body.ts and engine/rect.ts, the pad''s speeds to\nengine/motion.ts, and the Box type to scene/pallet/spec.ts. The context\ndrops the old queue, held case and strike, and the belt only takes cases\noff its end, since the engine now moves them along it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(components): add Sheet, Details gap and new utilities; move component styles to classes\n\nSheet is a panel at the side of the page that doesn''t block it: a manual\npopover that slides in, fills the screen on small ones, and keeps a\nSheetHeader and SheetFooter stuck to its edges with the gradient line.\nCard takes a `tone` for its edge colour, Details a `gap` that lays out its\ncontent without a wrapper, and Diagram a `className`.\n\nNew utilities cover what views were still writing as CSS: overflow,\ncursor, touch-action, user-select, overscroll-behavior, text-wrap,\naspect-ratio, a `none` gutter size with padding/margin shorthands, and\nblock padding from the frame inset. The stuck gradient line is now one\nmixin instead of a copy per view.\n\nComponents set properties through these classes where nothing else\nchanges them. Button puts bare text in a span, since CSS can''t see a text\nnode and a label beside an icon or spinner was drawn as a round icon\nbutton. Video no longer reads size variables that were never defined.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): setup panel on Sheet, with a log button and restart\n\nThe panel is now a Sheet, so its slide-in, sticky header and footer come\nfrom the component and the view keeps 12 lines of SCSS. The log opens from\nan icon button beside Preset and Manual and goes back to the tab it came\nfrom. The running preset''s play button restarts it.\n\nMarkup lost its layout wrappers: Layout sits on the elements that were\nalready there, settings are list items, and preset rows use Card''s header\nand action.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(views): semantic markup and utility classes across the app\n\nLayout-only divs are gone where an element was already there to take the\nlayout, and the rest became the element that means the thing: section,\narticle, figure, header, nav, aside, and lists for lists. Heading levels in\nimage-agent and music-visualiser are in order again.\n\nProperties a utility covers are classes now, and view SCSS went from about\n1,900 lines to 1,370; what''s left changes with state or a media query, or\nhas no utility. Multi-class names all go through classNames. The unused\nInfiniteScroll widget is removed.\n\nFixes: tree-of-life v15''s panel docks to the foot on smaller screens again\n(the utility it had to override always won), and v13 measures its stage\nonce the tree appears instead of keeping 960 by 640.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Keni Lam <admin@loci.world>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"37dcf8eec28b66febc091389b6b53c9fe21a2bd5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/git/trees/37dcf8eec28b66febc091389b6b53c9fe21a2bd5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/kenilam/Ki.CL/git/commits/66c4fc9a4e21a47e0bbdd52d8d3150c806713482","verification":{"payload":"tree 37dcf8eec28b66febc091389b6b53c9fe21a2bd5\nparent 041a7dd08008e089d51bd1196d3c84d40502f5ce\nauthor github-actions[bot] <41898282+github-actions[bot]@users.noreply.github.com> 1790579910 -0700\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790579910 -0700\n\nfeat(factory-arm): add a robot arm experiment that unloads a pallet (#433)\n\n* feat(factory-arm): add a robot arm experiment that unloads a pallet\n\nA new experiment at /experiments/factory-arm: a six-axis arm with a\nvacuum gripper, a stacked pallet, a buffer pallet and an outfeed belt,\nwith physics from @react-three/rapier. Clicking a case queues it for the\nbelt. Cases in the way of lifting it go to the buffer first and follow it\noff last in, first out. The buffer stacks them where they''re supported,\nheavy under light, looking ahead at the cases still to come.\n\nThe arm moves on closed-form IK with speed-limited joints and a wrist\nroll that squares the pad to each case. It goes into and out of contact\nin straight lines, slowing near contact, and swings freely only at\ntravel height.\n\nEight proximity sensors on the links find three obstacles it isn''t told\nabout. Each find rechecks the current move, and a blocked one goes round\nby A* over reachable, clear pad positions. With no way on, a held case\ngoes back to where it was picked; with no way back either, the arm stops\nand the light turns red. The view turns with OrbitControls.\n\nRapier is imported only inside the experiment, not through @/three, so\nits WASM doesn''t load with other pages.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): write context refs through the provider\n\noxlint 1.85 from develop flags `react/immutability` when a component\nassigns to `.current` on a ref it got from context, since the compiler\ncan''t tell it''s a ref. The arm, the sensing loop and the runner did that\nfor `command`, `joints`, `held`, `seeing` and `found`, and the case''s\n`useCallback` read `bodies.current`.\n\nThe provider now hands out a `write` object whose functions sit beside\nthe refs they change. `learn` adds new obstacles and bumps `found` once,\nand `track` registers a case''s body and returns what unregisters it.\nComponents still read the refs directly. Nothing else changes.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): keep buffer moves off places the arm can''t reach\n\nClicking a buried case sent the cases above it to a buffer spot next to\nthe crate. Once a sensor found the crate, setting the case down there was\nblocked, so the arm carried it back to where it came from. That left the\nclicked case buried again and the rest of the chain failed.\n\nA buffer spot now only counts if the case can go straight down onto it\nclear of every obstacle the arm already knows about. If a spot turns out\nblocked after the move has started, the case goes to the belt instead.\nTaking it back to the pallet is left for when the belt is blocked too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): shorter detours, and notices you can read\n\nThe beam hung over the front of the pallet, in the swing to the buffer,\nso every buffer move went round the back of the cell: about 4.7 m, and 4\nminutes for a nine-move chain. It now hangs low behind the pallet, out of\nthe swing, where it still stops one back-row case being lifted. Average\npaths from the top cases dropped from 4.1 m to 2.3 m, and detours run at\n0.6 m/s instead of 0.35.\n\nA queued case that can''t start because something it needs didn''t move\nwas dropped with nothing on screen. It now shows the same \"Skipped\"\nnotice as a case the arm gives up on.\n\nThe notices sat on the light floor as yellow and grey text. They now use\nthe frosted pane from the image agent''s header, tinted to 85% of the page\ncolour so they read over the cell.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): plan the whole move before picking a case up\n\nThe arm used to find out a case had nowhere to go only once it was\ncarrying it, then took it back or stopped. It now plans every move of a\njob with the obstacles it knows about before it starts, and again when\nthe pad reaches the case, since the sensors may have found more on the\nway. A case in the way tries the best few buffer spots, then the belt.\nIf nothing works it leaves the case where it is, lights the obstacles in\nthe way, says \"No clear path\" and calls off the rest of that click.\n\nA lift is the one move that can''t go round anything, so a click whose\nchain has a lift known to be blocked is refused straight away, and a\nchain found to have one mid-way stops before its next pick. Obstacles\nare only blamed for a move that is otherwise within reach; the travel\nheight is now capped at what the arm can reach above the pick and drop.\n\nCases moved out of the way now stay on the buffer instead of following\nthe clicked case to the belt, and can be clicked like any other.\nObstacles are grey and glow orange while they''re in the way of a move.\nA case queued for the belt glows green, and one being moved out of the\nway glows yellow until it''s on the buffer.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(factory-arm): don''t bury queued cases on the buffer\n\nA case moved out of the way could be stacked on a buffer case that was\nitself queued for the belt, so that case then had to be cleared again.\nBuffer spots that would be in the way of lifting any queued case now\nrank after every spot that wouldn''t, and are only used when there''s no\nother room.\n\nThe in-the-way test is the one picking uses (`covers` in cell.ts), so\n\"in the way\" means the same thing on both sides.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): keep carried cases off other cases, and pin notices\n\nA carried case could sweep into a stack and knock it. The planner only\navoided the three obstacles, and once the travel height was capped at the\narm''s reach, a far stack could stand taller than the carried case''s path.\nFree moves now treat the other cases on the pallets as solid too, so a\nway round goes over or past them. Moves into and out of contact still\nignore them, since the lift check already covers those. A box round the\narm and its load skips cases nowhere near, so planning stays around\n20-260 ms a job.\n\nIf the carried case still touches a case it isn''t being set down on, the\narm stops, lifts 10 cm, and plans the rest of the job again once the\nstruck case has settled. With no plan it takes the case back, and with\nno way back it stops red. The struck case glows orange.\n\nThe \"No clear path\" notice is now a warning badge floating over the case\nit''s about, following it if it moves. The page''s hidden live region\nstill announces it, since the scene is one image to a screen reader.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(badge): take meaning from a level prop, as Button does\n\nBadge put how it looks and what it means in one `variant` prop, copied\nfrom shadcn: `destructive`, `positive` and `warning` sat beside\n`secondary`, `outline` and `ghost`. Button already splits these into\n`variant` and `level`, so Badge now does the same.\n\n`variant` keeps `default`, `secondary`, `outline` and `ghost`. `level`\nis typed from Button''s own, `confirm`, `error`, `info` or `warning`: it\nfills the chip with that colour, or edges it for `outline` and `ghost`,\nwhich have no fill. `destructive` is gone, along with its duplicated\nrules. The level rules come after the themes so they win over the\nthemed fills.\n\nThe image agent''s allowance badge was the only one using the old\nvalues, and moves to `level`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(badge): make secondary tell apart from default\n\nSecondary looked the same as default. It now takes a faint kicl-backdrop fill\nand moves the badge''s colour to the text, as Button''s secondary does, so a\nlevel colours the text instead of the fill. Outline and ghost use the page''s\ntext colour. The level rules use two classes so they win over the theme\nrules.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): overhead camera, pallet overflow, and faster moves\n\nThe overhead camera sees obstacles near the pallets before the arm moves,\nso a job with no clear path is refused up front. The \"No clear path\" badge\nsits on the case it refers to.\n\nCases in the way now go to a clear place on the buffer, then on the incoming\npallet, and only bury a queued case when there is no other room. Queued cases\nthat a buffer place would bury are delivered first. Stacks also have to pass\na slenderness check, so the arm doesn''t build towers that tip over. A case\nselected while another is in the air sends that one to a new buffer place\ninstead of the belt.\n\nPlanning a job used to freeze the page for about 2.5s because it searched\nthe full incoming pallet on every job. That search now runs only when no\nbuffer place works, and without look-ahead.\n\nThe arm and gripper are slimmer. The arm swings and descends faster, and\nslows over the last 25cm before it touches a case. The scene has soft\nshadows, and the start view is lower and turned to show the stack and the\nbelt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(status): drop the outer Layout wrapper\n\nThe status div is now the root inside Animation, so a className passed to\nStatus lands on the element that holds the content, with no extra grid\naround it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): movable obstacles, and an arm that recovers on its own\n\nClick an obstacle to select it, then move it with the arrow keys, Shift+Up\nand Down, or the triangles round it. A move is refused if the obstacle would\noverlap a case, pallet, the belt, another obstacle or the arm, so it never\nknocks anything over. The arm knows where a moved obstacle stands.\n\nA case given up on glows red and is tried again once something it plans\nround has changed. A stopped arm checks the same way and carries on when it\nhas a way. The stopped notice is now a badge over the gripper, and both\nnotices are announced to screen readers from hidden live regions.\n\nThe belt has several drop spots, so an obstacle between the arm and the\nusual one no longer blocks every delivery. A spot whose set-down is blocked\nis skipped before any route search. A* weights the distance left more and\nhas a 200ms limit, so a search that finds nothing can''t freeze the page.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(components): add Segmented, and fix Select and CardFooter\n\nSegmented is a one-of-a-few choice on a shared track, built on native\nradios. The highlight slides to the chosen option with CSS anchor\npositioning, and without anchor support the chosen option is filled\ninstead.\n\nSelect centres its text in the styleable select, where the picker icon made\nthe box taller than a line and the text sat at the top. CardFooter renders a\nfooter element with padding top and bottom.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): setup panel, presets, second stack and activity log\n\nA panel over the stage sets up each run. Presets (built in, or saved in\nthis browser) run with a play button, and the active one is edged green.\nThe Manual tab drops obstacles onto the stage, sets the case layers and\nseed, and turns on a second incoming stack. Unsaved changes offer to save\nas a new preset, and starting over while the arm works asks first. The Log\ntab lists every step, coloured as the cases glow, with earlier runs folded\naway. The panel folds away on small screens and on desktop, and the camera\nkeeps the cell centred beside it.\n\nObstacles can be removed from a button over them, and the arm keeps its\nlinks off the cases it reaches over, clearing them first. Cases in the way\ngo only to the buffer. Waiting cases keep what blocks them lit.\n\nThis planner has reached its limits; a new engine follows.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): a new engine that plans each job whole\n\nThe old planner planned one move at a time against a physics world that\nkept shifting under it, and could end up holding a case with nowhere to\ntake it. The new engine, in engine/, models the cell as boxes with kinematic\ncases, and plans a whole job before the arm moves: each case in the way to\na stable place on the buffer, then the asked case to the belt. A refusal\nnames the case, why, and which obstacles are in the way.\n\nThe runner''s rules live in a state machine outside React (conductor.ts),\nwith tests. Plans are made in two web workers, so a slow one never drops\nframes. Collision checks skip links far from a solid, which cut the slowest\nplan from 2.2 s to 254 ms with the same results.\n\nThe arm plans round what the overhead camera and its sensors have seen, and\nfeels an obstacle neither saw. While an obstacle is moved it keeps going as\nlong as its way stays clear. Queued cases in the way go straight to the\nbelt, cases short of buffer room wait their turn, and refused cases are\ntried again after each drop. The shoulder, elbow and wrist now move\ntogether, so the pad no longer sags near full reach.\n\nThe log and the last run are kept in this browser, and the page opens on\nthe last run. The old engine still runs with ?classic, until it''s removed.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(factory-arm): remove the first engine\n\nThe new engine plans each job whole and has replaced it, so ?classic is\ngone, along with scene/grasp and the physics-driven cases in scene/boxes.\nThe pieces the new engine still used move with it: the arm''s collision\nmodel to engine/body.ts and engine/rect.ts, the pad''s speeds to\nengine/motion.ts, and the Box type to scene/pallet/spec.ts. The context\ndrops the old queue, held case and strike, and the belt only takes cases\noff its end, since the engine now moves them along it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(components): add Sheet, Details gap and new utilities; move component styles to classes\n\nSheet is a panel at the side of the page that doesn''t block it: a manual\npopover that slides in, fills the screen on small ones, and keeps a\nSheetHeader and SheetFooter stuck to its edges with the gradient line.\nCard takes a `tone` for its edge colour, Details a `gap` that lays out its\ncontent without a wrapper, and Diagram a `className`.\n\nNew utilities cover what views were still writing as CSS: overflow,\ncursor, touch-action, user-select, overscroll-behavior, text-wrap,\naspect-ratio, a `none` gutter size with padding/margin shorthands, and\nblock padding from the frame inset. The stuck gradient line is now one\nmixin instead of a copy per view.\n\nComponents set properties through these classes where nothing else\nchanges them. Button puts bare text in a span, since CSS can''t see a text\nnode and a label beside an icon or spinner was drawn as a round icon\nbutton. Video no longer reads size variables that were never defined.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* feat(factory-arm): setup panel on Sheet, with a log button and restart\n\nThe panel is now a Sheet, so its slide-in, sticky header and footer come\nfrom the component and the view keeps 12 lines of SCSS. The log opens from\nan icon button beside Preset and Manual and goes back to the tab it came\nfrom. The running preset''s play button restarts it.\n\nMarkup lost its layout wrappers: Layout sits on the elements that were\nalready there, settings are list items, and preset rows use Card''s header\nand action.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(views): semantic markup and utility classes across the app\n\nLayout-only divs are gone where an element was already there to take the\nlayout, and the rest became the element that means the thing: section,\narticle, figure, header, nav, aside, and lists for lists. Heading levels in\nimage-agent and music-visualiser are in order again.\n\nProperties a utility covers are classes now, and view SCSS went from about\n1,900 lines to 1,370; what''s left changes with state or a media query, or\nhas no utility. Multi-class names all go through classNames. The unused\nInfiniteScroll widget is removed.\n\nFixes: tree-of-life v15''s panel docks to the foot on smaller screens again\n(the utility it had to override always won),